<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Minibatch UOT-Reweighted Flow Matching (UOT-RFM)
</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
        <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Empirical or sampleable distributions <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, bandwidth <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, initial network <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, sinkhorn target marginal weight <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, weight power scale <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
        <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}\leftarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}\leftarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> // Source-fixed UOT

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
        <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> Training <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          Sample batches of size <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.i.d.</span> from the datasets: 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim\mu;\,\mathbf{x}_{1}\sim\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim\mu;\,\mathbf{x}_{1}\sim\nu</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau}^{u}\leftarrow\mathrm{UOT}(\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{0},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>UOT</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau}^{u}\leftarrow\mathrm{UOT}(\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{0},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> // Source-fixed UOT <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}=\tau</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u};\,t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u};\,t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{t}\leftarrow t\mathbf{x}_{1}+(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{t}\leftarrow t\mathbf{x}_{1}+(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{t},\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{t},\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          Calculate <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> from Equation (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Minibatch UOT Approximation ‣ 3.2 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Method ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{ours}(\theta)\leftarrow\hat{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-k}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x})-(\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{0})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{ours}(\theta)\leftarrow\hat{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-k}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x})-(\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{0})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
          <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\mathrm{Update}(\theta,\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{ours}(\theta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>Update</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\mathrm{Update}(\theta,\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{ours}(\theta))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
        <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
          
          
        <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Use of Large Language Models</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We acknowledge the use of a large language model (OpenAI ChatGPT) as a general-purpose writing assistant in the preparation of this work. Specifically, the LLM was used to improve grammar, phrasing, and clarity of the text. The authors take full responsibility for all scientific content presented in this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Unbalanced Optimal Transport</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the classical OT problem assumes an exact transport between two distributions <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=\mu,\pi_{1}=\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=\mu,\pi_{1}=\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.
This exact matching constraint makes OT sensitive to outliers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balaji et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Séjourné et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and vulnerable to class imbalance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eyring et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
To address these issues, the <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unbalanced Optimal Transport (UOT)</span> problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chizat et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liero et al., 2018</a>)</cite> relaxes the hard marginal constraints by introducing divergence penalties with regularization parameters <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1},\tau_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1},\tau_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C_{UOT}(\mu,\nu)=\inf_{\pi\in\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})}\Biggl[\int_{\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}}c(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\,d\pi(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})+\tau_{1}D_{\Psi_{1}}(\pi_{0}\|\mu)+\tau_{2}D_{\Psi_{2}}(\pi_{1}\|\nu)\Biggr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{UOT}(\mu,\nu)=\inf_{\pi\in\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})}\Biggl[\int_{\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}}c(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\,d\pi(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})+\tau_{1}D_{\Psi_{1}}(\pi_{0}\|\mu)+\tau_{2}D_{\Psi_{2}}(\pi_{1}\|\nu)\Biggr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of nonnegative finite measures on <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Psi_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Psi_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Psi_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Psi_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences generated by convex functions <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, penalizing discrepancies between the marginals <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0},\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0},\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Hence, in the UOT problem, the marginals are only <em id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximately matched</em> to <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are close to <math id="A2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the divergence penalties, rather than being exactly equal as in OT. Intuitively, UOT can be viewed as solving OT between divergence-relaxed marginals <math id="A2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0},\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0},\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target measures <math id="A2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\nu</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
This relaxation provides robustness to outliers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balaji et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and improved adaptability to class imbalance between <math id="A2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eyring et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the standard OT problem, the UOT problem also admits a <span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dual formulation</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallouët et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vacher &amp; Vialard, 2023</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C_{UOT}(\mu,\nu)=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}u(\mathbf{x})+v(\mathbf{y})\leq c(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\end{subarray}}\Biggl[\int_{\mathcal{X}}-\tau_{1}\Psi^{*}_{1}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{1}}u(\mathbf{x})\right)\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}-\tau_{2}\Psi^{*}_{2}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{2}}v(\mathbf{y})\right)\,d\nu(\mathbf{y})\Biggr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{UOT}(\mu,\nu)=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}u(\mathbf{x})+v(\mathbf{y})\leq c(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\end{subarray}}\Biggl[\int_{\mathcal{X}}-\tau_{1}\Psi^{*}_{1}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{1}}u(\mathbf{x})\right)\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}-\tau_{2}\Psi^{*}_{2}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{2}}v(\mathbf{y})\right)\,d\nu(\mathbf{y})\Biggr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous functions on <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <span id="A2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">convex conjugate</span> of <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(z)=\sup_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\{tz-f(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(z)=\sup_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\{tz-f(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow[-\infty,\infty]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow[-\infty,\infty]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This dual problem can be further simplified into a <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semi-dual</span> formulation by eliminating <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> via the optimality condition:</p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C_{UOT}(\mu,\nu)=\sup_{v\in\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Y})}\Biggl[\int_{\mathcal{X}}-\tau_{1}\Psi_{1}^{*}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{1}}v^{c}(\mathbf{x})\right)\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}-\tau_{2}\Psi^{*}_{2}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{2}}v(\mathbf{y})\right)\,d\nu(\mathbf{y})\Biggr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{UOT}(\mu,\nu)=\sup_{v\in\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Y})}\Biggl[\int_{\mathcal{X}}-\tau_{1}\Psi_{1}^{*}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{1}}v^{c}(\mathbf{x})\right)\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})+\int_{\mathcal{Y}}-\tau_{2}\Psi^{*}_{2}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{2}}v(\mathbf{y})\right)\,d\nu(\mathbf{y})\Biggr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-transform of <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{c}(\mathbf{x})=\underset{\mathbf{y}\in\mathcal{Y}}{\inf}\,\big(c(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-v(\mathbf{y})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{c}(\mathbf{x})=\underset{\mathbf{y}\in\mathcal{Y}}{\inf}\,\big(c(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})-v(\mathbf{y})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the optimal potential <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the relationship between the marginals of the optimal UOT plan <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original source and target distributions can be expressed using the optimal UOT potential <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> from the semi-dual problem:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallouët et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vacher &amp; Vialard, 2023</a>)</cite>)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> be a solution of the dual formulation of the UOT problem between the source distribution <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and the target distribution <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the marginal distributions of the optimal UOT plan <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\pi^{u}_{0}(\mathbf{x})\;=\;(\Psi_{1}^{*})^{\prime}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{1}}v^{c}(\mathbf{x})\right)\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})\quad\text{and}\quad d\pi^{u}_{1}(\mathbf{y})\;=\;(\Psi_{2}^{*})^{\prime}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{2}}v(\mathbf{y})\right)\,d\nu(\mathbf{y})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\pi^{u}_{0}(\mathbf{x})\;=\;(\Psi_{1}^{*})^{\prime}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{1}}v^{c}(\mathbf{x})\right)\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})\quad\text{and}\quad d\pi^{u}_{1}(\mathbf{y})\;=\;(\Psi_{2}^{*})^{\prime}\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{\tau_{2}}v(\mathbf{y})\right)\,d\nu(\mathbf{y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proofs of theorem</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the proof of our bias correction theorem (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Proposed Method ‣ 3.2 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Method ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) from the main text. Our proof builds on three key lemmas for the standard flow matching model, originally established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al. (2022)</a></cite>, which we restate here for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma C.1</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Theorem 3.1)<span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The marginal vector field <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> generates the probability path <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from initial conditions <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="A3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=\int p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{z})\pi(\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z},\quad\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}):=\mathbb{E}_{\pi(\mathbf{z})}\left[\frac{\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{z})p_{t}(\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{z})}{p_{t}(\mathbf{x})}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{t}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x}_{t})}\left[\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{z})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})=\int p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{z})\pi(\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z},\quad\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}):=\mathbb{E}_{\pi(\mathbf{z})}\left[\frac{\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{z})p_{t}(\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{z})}{p_{t}(\mathbf{x})}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{t}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x}_{t})}\left[\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{z})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma C.2</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Theorem 3.2)<span id="A3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, then, up to a constant independent of <math id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CFM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CFM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CFM}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Flow Matching ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq.<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Flow Matching ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) are equal, and hence</span></p>
<table id="A3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CFM}}(\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FM</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CFM</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CFM}}(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma C.3</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Proposition 3.4)<span id="A3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the initial sample coupling be <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and define the conditional vector probability path and vector field as in Eq. <a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Flow Matching ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Then, the corresponding marginal probability path <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the boundary conditions <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=\pi_{0}*\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}|0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=\pi_{0}*\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}|0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=\pi_{1}*\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}|0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=\pi_{1}*\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}|0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the convolution operator. Furthermore, assuming regularity
properties of <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0},q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0},q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the optimal transport plan <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the marginal path <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and field <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> minimize (7), i.e., <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> solves the dynamic optimal transport problem between <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\to\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\to\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\to\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\to\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we provide a formal statement of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Proposed Method ‣ 3.2 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Method ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and provide its proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C.4</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Proposed Method ‣ 3.2 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Method ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>)<span id="A3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> be the optimal source-fixed UOT coupling between <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}=\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}=\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that its target marginal satisfies <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\ll\pi_{\tau}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>≪</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\ll\pi_{\tau}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Training a flow matching model with <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the biased distribution <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=\pi^{u}_{1}\neq\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=\pi^{u}_{1}\neq\nu</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eyring et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, applying the first-order correction (our method with <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>) recovers the true target distribution <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="A3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ours,k=1}}({\bm{\theta}})=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{z}),\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\,\,\left[s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>ours</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ours,k=1}}({\bm{\theta}})=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{z}),\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\,\,\left[s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the majority score <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(\cdot):=\frac{d\pi^{u}_{1}}{d\nu}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(\cdot):=\frac{d\pi^{u}_{1}}{d\nu}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, UOT-RFM with correction order <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> generates a distribution <math id="A3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\propto s_{\tau}^{-k}\pi^{u}_{\tau,1}=s_{\tau}^{-(k-1)}\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\propto s_{\tau}^{-k}\pi^{u}_{\tau,1}=s_{\tau}^{-(k-1)}\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As an overview, the proof relies on two observations: (1) training with <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=\pi^{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=\pi^{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the biased UOT marginal (Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem B.1 (( , )). ‣ Appendix B Unbalanced Optimal Transport ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) and (2) importance reweighting with <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> corrects this bias, since <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=s_{\tau}^{-1}\pi^{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=s_{\tau}^{-1}\pi^{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by the Radon–Nikodym derivative. Substituting this correction into the conditional flow matching loss yields Eq. <a href="#A3.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ Theorem C.4 (Theorem ). ‣ Appendix C Proofs of theorem ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>, and hence the generated distribution recovers <math id="A3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Formally, Lemma <a href="#A3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma C.3 ( ( ) , Proposition 3.4). ‣ Appendix C Proofs of theorem ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a> shows that training a flow matching model with the optimal source-fixed UOT coupling <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="A3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{UOT-CFM}}({\bm{\theta}})=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u},\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>UOT</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>CFM</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{UOT-CFM}}({\bm{\theta}})=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u},\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">yields a flow matching model whose boundary conditions converge to <math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\to\pi_{\tau,0}^{u},p_{1}\to\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\to\pi_{\tau,0}^{u},p_{1}\to\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem B.1 (( , )). ‣ Appendix B Unbalanced Optimal Transport ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, we have <math id="A3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau,0}^{u}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau,0}^{u}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}\neq\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}\neq\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the UOT-CFM model generates a biased distribution.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, we now show that our UOT-RFM model with the first-order correction recovers the true target distribution. From Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem B.1 (( , )). ‣ Appendix B Unbalanced Optimal Transport ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, we have <math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau}^{u}\ll\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≪</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau}^{u}\ll\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, so the Radon–Nikodym derivative exists and corresponds to the majority score. By our assumption <math id="A3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\ll\pi_{\tau}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>≪</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\ll\pi_{\tau}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows <math id="A3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=s_{\tau}^{-1}\pi^{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=s_{\tau}^{-1}\pi^{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ours,k=1}}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>ours</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ours,k=1}}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{z}),\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\left[s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{z}),\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\left[s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}}\left[s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\left[\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{z}\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}}\left[s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})}\left[\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{x}_{t})-\mathbf{u}_{t|\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|\mathbf{z})\|_{2}^{2}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that the reweighted coupling <math id="A3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> has the true target distribution <math id="A3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> as its marginal.</span></p>
<table id="A3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\,d\mathbf{x}_{0}=s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}(\mathbf{x}_{1})=\nu(\mathbf{x}_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\pi_{\tau}^{u}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\,d\mathbf{x}_{0}=s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{1})^{-1}\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}(\mathbf{x}_{1})=\nu(\mathbf{x}_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then, following a similar argument as in the UOT-CFM case, our UOT-RFM model with the first-order correction (<math id="A3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>) recovers the true target distribution <math id="A3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we specifically employ the source-fixed UOT coupling to ensure that the source marginal <math id="A3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau,0}^{u}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau,0}^{u}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math> matches exactly with the initial distribution of the flow matching model. More generally, by a similar argument, UOT-RFM with correction order <math id="A3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> generates a distribution <math id="A3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\propto s_{\tau}^{-k}\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\propto s_{\tau}^{-k}\pi_{\tau,1}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to a normalizing constant.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generative Models for Long-tailed Data</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many real-world datasets follow long-tailed distributions, where a few dominant classes (Head class) contain the majority of samples, while numerous minority classes (Tail class) consist of a smaller number of samples. Generative modeling for long-tailed distributions often fails to learn the tail classes, resulting in low-diversity, low-quality samples. To address this, several GAN-based approaches have been proposed. CB-GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rangwani et al., 2021</a>)</cite> introduces a regularizer by utilizing a pretrained classifier to balance class learning. gSR-GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rangwani et al., 2022</a>)</cite> mitigates tail-class mode collapse through group spectral regularization.
UTLO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khorram et al., 2024</a>)</cite> encourages head-to-tail knowledge sharing by combining an unconditional low-resolution generator with a conditional high-resolution generator.
More recently, diffusion-based methods have been developed for long-tailed generation. CBDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and LTDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> improve tail-class quality by transferring knowledge from head to tail classes. In parallel, test-time guidance methods have been introduced. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Um et al. (2024)</a></cite> uses proxy class labels to guide minority sampling. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Um &amp; Ye (2024)</a></cite> leverages a self-consistent minority score for minority guidance. In contrast, our method is label-free and corrects majority bias directly during training by leveraging the geometric structure of data via Unbalanced Optimal Transport. Furthermore, to the best of our knowledge, our approach is the first flow matching model designed for long-tailed distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coupling in Flow Matching</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key design choice in flow matching is the coupling between source and target samples. The original framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>)</cite> employs independent coupling. This independent coupling often produces curved trajectories due to flow crossing, increasing numerical errors and sampling cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. To mitigate this, recent works proposed OT-based couplings between mini-batches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pooladian et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or trajectory refinement via pretrained models in Rectified Flow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eyring et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduced the UOT-based couplings to the image-to-image translation, motivated by its adaptability to class imbalance. Model-Aligned Coupling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> dynamically adjusts couplings during training, aligning them with the flow matching model being trained. However, these approaches do not tackle the majority bias in long-tailed generation. Our work complements them by combining UOT-based couplings with a reweighting mechanism that explicitly mitigates majority oversampling. This is the first attempt to utilize the density ratio between the target distribution and the UOT coupling as the majority score. We employ this score to weight the flow matching objective for long-tailed generation and formally characterize the resulting generated distribution (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Proposed Method ‣ 3.2 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Method ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides the specific implementation details for our experiments on the CIFAR-10 and 2D synthetic datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Experiments on CIFAR-10</h3>

<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two datasets for our image generation experiments: the standard CIFAR-10/100 dataset and their long-tailed version, CIFAR-10-LT and CIFAR-100-LT. The CIFAR-10-LT/100-LT are generated to simulate class imbalance, following an exponential decay distribution. The number of samples <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each class <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the formula <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}=\lfloor n_{\max}\cdot\mathcal{I}^{\frac{i}{M-1}}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}=\lfloor n_{\max}\cdot\mathcal{I}^{\frac{i}{M-1}}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\{10,100\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>10,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\{10,100\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of classes, <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples in the largest class, and the imbalance factor <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.01.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Network Architecture</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ the U-Net architecture provided in the <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torchcfm</span> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al. (2024)</a></cite>, without any modifications. The architecture uses four resolution levels with two residual blocks per level in both encoder and decoder, linked by skip connections at matching scales. Each block uses 3<math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3 convolutions with Group Normalization, SiLU activations, and dropout. Down-sampling is performed by stride-2 convolutions, and up-sampling uses nearest-neighbor interpolation followed by a 3<math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3 convolution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Details</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments on CIFAR-10/100 follow the default settings of <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torchcfm</span>. We use the <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dopri5</span> ODE solver. For optimization, we use the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is trained for a total of 400,000 iterations with a batch size of 128. Data preprocessing includes <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip()</span> and normalization of pixel values to the range <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> using <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transforms.Normalize(mean=[0.5, 0.5, 0.5], std=[0.5, 0.5, 0.5])</span>. For stable training, we apply a warmup schedule for the first 5,000 iterations, linearly increasing the learning rate from 0 to its target value, and use gradient clipping with an L2-norm threshold of 1.0. For Unbalanced Optimal Transport (UOT), the entropy regularization parameter <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the source marginal relaxation weight <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to infinity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Method Details</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training process of our proposed method is as follows: (1) Sample mini-batches from each distribution. (2) Compute the coupling (transport plan) between the two mini-batches. (3) Determine the weight for each sample based on the computed transport plan. (4) Estimate the vector fields by feeding the coupled sample pairs into the U-Net and compute the weighted loss. (5) Update the network parameters via backpropagation. The specifics of each coupling method are as follows:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ICFM:</span> Uses an independent coupling, assuming the two distributions are independent.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OT-CFM:</span> Computes the transport plan <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> using the <span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pot.emd</span> function and samples pairs according to the normalized probability distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UOT-CFM:</span> Computes the transport plan <math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> using the <span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pot.unbalanced.sinkhorn_knopp_unbalanced</span> function and samples pairs based on the normalized probabilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UOT-RFM:</span> Also uses <span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pot.unbalanced.sinkhorn_knopp_unbalanced</span>, but samples only one target for each source sample from the normalized transport plan <math id="A5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\tau}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\tau}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The sample weights are calculated using the column sums of the transport plan <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to the empirical measure of the target distribution, denoted as <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weight <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}^{-k}(\mathbf{x}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}^{-k}(\mathbf{x}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as Eq. <a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Majority Score ‣ 3.2 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Method ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The final loss function is the weighted mean squared error (MSE) between the vector fields: <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}}\left[s_{\tau}^{-k}(\mathbf{x}_{1})\|\mathbf{v}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})-\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\sim\pi_{\tau}^{u}}\left[s_{\tau}^{-k}(\mathbf{x}_{1})\|\mathbf{v}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})-\mathbf{u}_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the quality of the generated images, we use the Fréchet Inception Distance (FID), Precision, and Recall. FID scores are calculated using the <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cleanfid</span> library. For evaluation against the standard CIFAR-10/100 dataset, we use the library’s built-in feature statistics. For CIFAR-10-LT and CIFAR-100-LT, the real data statistics are computed from a long-tailed dataset generated in the same manner as the training set. Precision and Recall are measured based on a widely-used implementation<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/blandocs/improved-precision-and-recall-metric-pytorch" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/blandocs/improved-precision-and-recall-metric-pytorch</a></span></span></span>, where the real data distribution is also generated identically to the training setup.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Class Distribution</h3>

<figure id="A6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/class_ratio_dist.svg" id="A6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/196;" width="548" height="196"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="A6.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generated class distribution on CIFAR-100-LT</span> with <math id="A6.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ F.1 Class Distribution ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> visualizes the distribution of the CIFAR-100-LT dataset and the classification results of samples generated by various generative models trained on the CIFAR-100-LT dataset. The classification was performed using a pre-trained classification model (RepVGG-A2) trained on CIFAR100. Ideally, a generative model should produce samples that follow the data’s class distribution; however, the results show a notable divergence. Our model, UOT-RFM (<math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=2.0,k=16.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16.0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=2.0,k=16.0</annotation></semantics></math>), can be seen in Figure <a href="#A6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ F.1 Class Distribution ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> to better follow the class distribution of the data compared to the baselines I-CFM and OT-CFM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/class_ratio_normdiff_cifar10LT.svg" id="A6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/157;" width="439" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span><span id="A6.F8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Signed Normalized Class Ratio Error (Signed NCRE) between generated sample and CIFAR-10-LT. The average NCREs are 0.84 for I-CFM, 1.02 for OT-CFM, and 0.40 for our method.
</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#A6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ F.1 Class Distribution ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we present a visualization of the Signed Normalized Class Ratio Error (Signed NCRE), calculated as <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r_{gen,i}-r_{data,i}}{r_{data,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r_{gen,i}-r_{data,i}}{r_{data,i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each class, where a lower absolute value implies better alignment with the data distribution. Our analysis reveals that both I-CFM and OT-CFM exhibit significant discrepancies, while our proposed method shows notably better class distribution alignment (with <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1.0,k=10.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10.0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1.0,k=10.0</annotation></semantics></math>).
This is quantitatively supported by the mean of NCRE, which are 0.84, 1.02, and 0.40 for I-CFM, OT-CFM, and our method, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/class_ratio_normdiff_zoom.svg" id="A6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/196;" width="548" height="196"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="A6.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Signed Normalized Class Ratio Error (Signed NCRE) between generated sample and CIFAR-100-LT</span>. The average NCREs are 2.41 for I-CFM, 2.79 for OT-CFM, and 1.82 for our method.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We visualized the Signed NCRE of CIFAR-100-LT for each class in Figure <a href="#A6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ F.1 Class Distribution ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
While I-CFM and OT-CFM often show large discrepancies, our method (<math id="A6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=2.0,k=16.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16.0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=2.0,k=16.0</annotation></semantics></math>) shows a much similar class distribution to that of data.
The mean NCRE scores are 2.41 for I-CFM, 2.79 for OT-CFM, and 1.82 for our method, indicating superior distributional fidelity. The two zoomed-in subplots further illustrate the advantage of our approach, particularly in modeling minority classes. In the right subplot (classes 60–80), our method shows substantially lower normalized errors, while I-CFM and OT-CFM display large discrepancies for these rare classes. These visual and quantitative results together confirm that our method more accurately preserves the original class distribution, especially in the challenging tail regions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Classwise Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL)</h3>

<figure id="A6.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/classwise_nll_cifar10LT.svg" id="A6.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/177;" width="494" height="177"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span><span id="A6.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class-wise negative log-likelihood of CIFAR-10-LT</span> data samples on each model. Our method achieves an overall average NLL of 3.88, while I-CFM and OT-CFM achieve 4.02 and 4.06, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To measure how faithfully each model adheres to the distribution, we also measure the Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL). The log-likelihood of data sample <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by solving an ODE at each data sample as shown in the following equation:</p>
<table id="A6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{1})=\log p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})-\int_{1}^{0}\text{div}_{t}(\mathbf{v})dt," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mtext>div</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{1})=\log p_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})-\int_{1}^{0}\text{div}_{t}(\mathbf{v})dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where integral of divergence is approximated by accumulation of ODE simulation. In our experiments, we use the BPD(bits per dimension) for comparison as <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{NLL}={-\log p_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{1})}/{(\log(2)\cdot d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>NLL</mtext><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{NLL}={-\log p_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{1})}/{(\log(2)\cdot d)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes dimension of data.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further analyze our model’s performance on long-tailed distributions, we visualize the class-wise negative log-likelihood (NLL) on the CIFAR-10-LT (imbalance factor <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>) dataset in Figure <a href="#A6.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ F.2 Classwise Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Our method achieves the lowest (best) NLL across all ten classes, from index 0 to 9, which indicates that our model can more accurately estimate the distribution within each class. Our method achieves an overall mean NLL of 3.88, while I-CFM and OT-CFM achieve 4.02 and 4.06, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/classwise_nll_normdiff_cifar10LT.svg" id="A6.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/177;" width="494" height="177"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span><span id="A6.F11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalized difference of class-wise NLL between our model and the baselines on CIFAR-10-LT</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a more detailed view of this improvement, Figure <a href="#A6.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ F.2 Classwise Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows the normalized difference in NLL between our method and the baselines, I-CFM and OT-CFM. The results demonstrate that our NLL values are consistently lower than the baselines, with improvements ranging from a minimum of approximately 2.8% (class 0 and 2, from I-CFM) to a maximum of over 5.0% (class 7 and 9, from OT-CFM). Additionally, the difference is more pronounced in the tail classes. This provides clear evidence that our approach is more effective at capturing the true data distribution, particularly for the underrepresented classes in a long-tailed setting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/classwise_nll.svg" id="A6.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/196;" width="548" height="196"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span><span id="A6.F12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class-wise negative log-likelihood of CIFAR-100-LT</span> data samples on each model. Our method achieves an overall average NLL of 3.94, while I-CFM and OT-CFM achieve 4.08 and 4.10, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we also measured and visualized the class-wise negative log-likelihood for CIFAR-100-LT (imbalance factor <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>). Figure <a href="#A6.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ F.2 Classwise Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> visualizes the average NLL(lower is better) for each class and the mean NLL. Our model achieves 3.94 mean NLL which is lower than I-CFM(4.08) and OT-CFM(4.10) in the CIFAR-100-LT dataset.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A6.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25713v1/figure/class_distribution/classwise_nll_normdiff.svg" id="A6.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/196;" width="548" height="196"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span><span id="A6.F13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalized difference of class-wise NLL between our model and the baselines on CIFAR-100-LT</span>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also show the normalized difference between our model and the competing models in Figure <a href="#A6.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ F.2 Classwise Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results on Majority Bias ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> to emphasize the gap. Our results show that our method achieves a lower(better) NLL than I-CFM and OT-CFM for most classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Additional Ablation Studies</h2>

