</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix G Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Reweighted Flow Matching via Unbalanced OT for Label-free Long-tailed Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents an ablation study on the effects of the correction order <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and the marginal matching strength <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. All models were trained on the CIFAR-10-LT dataset. The “LT<math id="A7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>LT” columns show FID scores measured against the CIFAR-10-LT dataset itself, assessing fidelity to the training distribution. The “LT<math id="A7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>Balanced” columns show FID scores using the class-balanced CIFAR10 dataset as a reference, evaluating the generation of a balanced distribution. First, analyzing the LT<math id="A7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>LT results, the task is to faithfully replicate the long-tailed training distribution. In this scenario, a clear trend emerges: performance consistently improves as the correction order <math id="A7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases. For any given value of <math id="A7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, a larger <math id="A7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> leads to a lower (better) FID score. For example, when <math id="A7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, the FID score monotonically decreases from 13.77 at <math id="A7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> to a superior 11.37 at <math id="A7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8.0</annotation></semantics></math>. This indicates that overcorrection (<math id="A7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) is consistently beneficial, helping the model to more accurately estimate and represent the target long-tailed marginal distribution. In contrast, the LT<math id="A7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>Balanced setting reveals a more complex trade-off. Here, a smaller <math id="A7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 2.0) enables a strong corrective weight but diminishes the sampling probability of minor classes. Conversely, a larger <math id="A7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 6.0) improves the sampling of these classes but flattens the weights, reducing their corrective impact. This necessitates a higher correction order <math id="A7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to induce overcorrection. For instance, with <math id="A7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=4.0</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing <math id="A7.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from 1.0 to 8.0 improves the FID score from 24.94 to 24.37. However, excessive overcorrection can overshoot the balanced target, as seen for <math id="A7.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, where the FID score worsens from 24.54 (<math id="A7.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4.0</annotation></semantics></math>) to 24.76 (<math id="A7.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8.0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A7.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span><span id="A7.T6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FID (<math id="A7.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) scores for CIFAR-10 based datasets</span>. The <span id="A7.T6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">boldface</span> and <span id="A7.T6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span> values indicate the best and second-best performance. * indicates the best performance of UOT-RFM.
</figcaption>
<table id="A7.T6.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A7.T6.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A7.T6.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A7.T6.8.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">CIFAR-10</td>
<td id="A7.T6.8.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">CIFAR-10-LT (0.01)</td>
